# Bird-eye map controls

In bird-eye view the stream behaves like a top-down map: drag-pan to move the player, no look-rotation. When a bottom sheet (Directions / POI) is open over the map, Kit also tilts the camera further down so the player marker pulls visually upward, clearing the sheet.

This file documents three tightly-coupled behaviors that all share the same constraint: **keep the area above the bottom sheet drag-pannable at all times, even while the sheet is animating in/out.**

| Behavior | Lives in | Trigger |
|---|---|---|
| Camera-relative drag-pan ("grab the world") | `bird_eye_usd_translate_mover.py` + `player_input_controller.py` | Pointer drag on the stream when `viewType === "birdEye"` |
| Map rotation (two-finger twist / middle-mouse / Shift+LMB) | `bird_eye_yaw_service.py` + `AppStream.tsx` | `birdEyeYawDelta` events from web |
| Cinematic tilt while panel is open | `bird_eye_framer.py` + `player_core_extension/extension.py` | `birdEyeFrameTarget` / `birdEyeFrameRestore` events from web |
| Map-marker icon swap mirroring the panel swap arrow | `BirdEyeRouteOverlay.tsx` reads `nav.mapMarkerDirectionsSwapped` | User clicks the swap button in the directions panel |

---

## 1. Drag-pan ("grab the world")

### Pipeline

```
Frontend pointer drag on #remote-video
  → sendCustomMessage('touchLookDelta', { dx, dy }) while dragging
  → sendCustomMessage('touchLookEnd', {}) on pointer-up after pan drag
    → Kit TouchLookHandler — drag + post-release pan coast (bird-eye branch)
      → PlayerInputController.update() — branches on view type:
          * firstPerson: tick(dt) → camera RotateXYZ  (look + short coast)
          * birdEye:    tick_bird_eye_pan(dt) → movement_state forward/right (brief coast)
            → BirdEyeUsdTranslateMover.drive() — writes player prim translate
```

### Why a custom XZ basis (not the camera's local rotation)

`TeleportService` zeroes the camera's local rotation when entering bird-eye and parents the spawn rotation onto the player body instead. Reading `camera.RotateXYZOp[1]` therefore returns `0` in bird-eye, which would lock pan directions to world `+Z` / `-X` regardless of where the user is actually looking after a runtime camera-angle change.

`BirdEyeUsdTranslateMover.drive()` instead derives the basis from `cam_xf.ComputeLocalToWorldTransform()`:

```python
fwd_w = cam_m.TransformDir(Gf.Vec3d(0.0, 0.0, -1.0))   # USD camera local -Z = forward
rgt_w = cam_m.TransformDir(Gf.Vec3d(1.0, 0.0, 0.0))    # USD camera local +X = right
fwd_xz = Gf.Vec3d(fwd_w[0], 0.0, fwd_w[2])             # project onto ground plane
rgt_xz = Gf.Vec3d(rgt_w[0], 0.0, rgt_w[2])
```

`ComputeLocalToWorldTransform` picks up rotation at any level of the transform hierarchy, so the basis stays correct after spawn and after any runtime tilt (including the cinematic tilt below). A fallback path uses the controller's tracked yaw if the matrix can't be read.

### Inverted ("grab the world") sign convention

In `PlayerInputController.update()` the bird-eye branch negates the touch deltas before writing them into `movement_state`:

```python
movement_state["forward"] = -float(t_pitch)   # drag UP   → player moves DOWN  (world slides up under finger)
movement_state["right"]   = -float(t_yaw)     # drag LEFT → player moves RIGHT (world slides left under finger)
```

This is the standard mobile-map convention: the world feels anchored to the finger/cursor, not the player. The first-person branch keeps the original (non-inverted) signs.

### Throughput tuning

`bird_eye_speed_multiplier = 8.0` inside `BirdEyeUsdTranslateMover.drive()` is the only knob distinguishing bird-eye pan speed from first-person walking speed. Changing it scales pan speed without affecting WASD / joystick pace.

### Post-release pan coast

On pointer-up after a pan drag, the web sends **`touchLookEnd`** (same event as first-person drag-look). `TouchLookHandler` arms **`_be_coast_armed`** and `PlayerInputController` keeps calling **`tick_bird_eye_pan(dt)`** until velocity decays — much shorter than FP look coast:

| Constant (`touch_look_handler.py`) | Default | Compare to FP |
|---|---|---|
| `BIRD_EYE_COAST_DECAY_PER_SEC` | `95.0` | Very short glide; FP look uses `12.0` (longer) |
| `BIRD_EYE_COAST_STOP_VEL` | `0.18` | FP uses `0.25` deg/s |

Decay math: `ease_helpers.apply_exponential_coast`. Rotation drag / two-finger twist do **not** send `touchLookEnd` for coast — they use **`birdEyeYawEnd`** (§2b).

