## 2b. Map rotation — `BirdEyeYawService`

Spec: yaw-only map rotation, pivoting around the player marker's screen position (the player body's world XZ — see §2b "Why we rotate the player body" below for why this is *effectively* the `camera_self` pivot the spec asked for). Reset on every bird-eye entry. Web-driven by **two-finger twist** on touch and **middle-mouse drag** (or **Shift + left-mouse drag** as a trackpad fallback) on desktop. Pinch zoom and twist run **simultaneously** during a single two-finger gesture (standard mobile-map UX).

### Why we rotate the **player body** around world +Y (not the camera's local Z)

The first iteration of this service wrote the camera's local `RotateXYZ.z` on the assumption that "in bird-eye the camera looks straight down, so its local Z axis is roughly world up." That's almost true — but the player body's spawn rotation `(159°, -45°, 180°)` tilts the camera's local Z far enough off vertical that the rotation reads as a **horizon roll** (buildings tip diagonally), not a map rotation.

`BirdEyeYawService` instead composes the new yaw onto the **player body's** existing `xformOp:rotateXYZ`, applying it at the world (parent) level:

```python
baseline_rot = Rz(bz) * Ry(by) * Rx(bx)            # body's current rotation
yaw_world    = Ry(yaw_deg)                          # rotate around world +Y
target       = yaw_world * baseline_rot             # PREPEND in matrix order
new_euler    = target.Decompose(Vec3d X, Y, Z)      # back to USD RotateXYZ
```

Because the camera is parented under the body, this rotates the bird-eye view around the body's pivot (= the camera's world XZ position) — the player marker stays put on screen and everything else swings around it. `BirdEyeUsdTranslateMover.drive()` derives its pan basis from `cam_xf.ComputeLocalToWorldTransform()`, so drag-pan automatically follows the new heading without any extra wiring.

### Baseline capture — drift-free repeated deltas

The body's Euler at the **first** `apply_delta` call is captured into `_baseline_euler`. Every subsequent call recomputes the body rotation as `Ry(total_yaw) * baseline`. Read-modify-decompose-write per call would slowly drift through Gf's decomposition rounding; recomposing from the baseline is exact for any total yaw.

### Gimbal guard

Decomposition is ambiguous when the body's pitch is near ±90°. The bird-eye spawn pitch is ~-45° so the guard never trips in practice — but if a future spawn point is added inside the band, the service drops the apply (with a one-line log) rather than emit a decomposition that snaps the camera around. Guard width: `_GIMBAL_GUARD_DEG = 2.0`.

### Defensive read-modify-write on the framer

`BirdEyeFramer` only writes the X (pitch) component, but its `_write_pitch_deg` was updated to read-modify-write so it preserves Y/Z. This is purely defensive — `BirdEyeYawService` no longer touches the camera, but if a future bird-eye service ever writes Y or Z on the camera's `RotateXYZ`, the framer won't clobber it.

### Lifecycle

```
Web pointer pipeline (`useRemoteVideoPointerGestures.ts`, bird-eye only):
  Two fingers down                → twistLastAngleRad = atan2(p2 - p1)
  Two-finger move                 → angle delta → birdEyeYawDelta; pinch → birdEyeZoomLevel
  Two-finger lift (pinch end)     → birdEyeYawEnd if twist moved this gesture
  Middle mouse / Shift+LMB drag   → dx * 0.4°/px → birdEyeYawDelta
  Rotate-drag pointer up          → birdEyeYawEnd

Kit (`extension.py`):
  birdEyeYawDelta → BirdEyeYawService.queue_delta(deg)
  birdEyeYawEnd   → BirdEyeYawService.arm_coast()

PlayerUpdateLoop each frame:
  BirdEyeYawService.tick(dt) — apply queued delta, then optional post-release coast

`BirdEyeYawService.apply_delta` / baseline / gimbal guard unchanged; coast tuning:

| Constant | Default |
|---|---|
| `COAST_DECAY_PER_SEC` | `95.0` (very short glide) |
| `COAST_STOP_VEL_DEG_S` | `1.2` |

SettingsWatchers on view-type change:
  - yaw.snap_restore() → restore baseline Euler, drop accumulator + coast
```

### Sensitivity & sign conventions

| Channel | Constant / formula | Sign behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| Mouse drag | `ROTATE_DEG_PER_PX = 0.4` | `dx > 0` (drag right) → positive yaw delta → camera rotates left → map appears to rotate right under the cursor ("grab the world" convention, matches drag-pan sign inversion in §1) |
| Two-finger twist | 1:1 — `dRad → deg` | Fingers rotate clockwise on screen → negative yaw delta → map appears to rotate clockwise (mobile-map convention) |

The `angleBetweenRad` helper negates screen Y (which grows downward) so it returns a CCW-positive mathematical angle, and `wrapAngle` collapses the ±π discontinuity into the shortest signed delta — without it, crossing the discontinuity once per gesture would emit a ~360° spike that snaps the camera around.

A sub-degree threshold (`Math.abs(deltaDeg) < 0.05`) is applied web-side before sending so a "still" finger doesn't flood the data channel with micro-noise.

### Pinch + twist coexistence

On a single two-finger gesture, `handlePointerMove` updates the same `pinchPointers` map, recomputes both the distance (zoom) and the angle (yaw) per frame, and dispatches both events. The two Kit services are independent (`BirdEyeZoomService` writes the camera's `focalLength` on the session layer; `BirdEyeYawService` writes the player body's `RotateXYZ` Euler) so simultaneous pinch + twist Just Works.

### Reset on bird-eye exit

Per spec the user sees an axis-aligned map every time bird-eye is re-entered. `SettingsWatchers._on_view_type_changed` calls `yaw.snap_restore()` on every view-type change (alongside the existing `zoom.snap_restore()`). It restores the body's baseline Euler (captured on the first apply of this session) and drops the accumulator. If the user never rotated in this bird-eye session the baseline was never captured and the call is a no-op.

### Rotation drag never produces a tap

The rotation drag (middle mouse / Shift+LMB) sets `isRotateDrag = true` on pointerdown and `isDragging = true` immediately (skipping the `DRAG_THRESHOLD_PX` tap window). On pointerup, the `wasRotateDrag` branch returns *before* the navigation pick-request branch — otherwise releasing the middle button would silently fire a `birdEyePin` pick at the cursor location.

